-- title:
--   Commensurability of valuations on a field algebraic over ℚ
-- statement:
--   Let $F$ be a field equipped with a $\mathbb{Q}$-algebra structure such that $F$ is algebraic over $\mathbb{Q}$, and let $\mathcal{O}$ be a valuation subring of $F$, with associated valuation $v =$ `O.valuation` taking values in the value group with zero `O.ValueGroup`. Let $\pi_0 \in F$ satisfy $v(\pi_0) \neq 0$ and $v(\pi_0) < 1$, and let $x \in F$ be nonzero. Then there exist a natural number $m$ with $0 < m$ and an integer $k$ such that $$v(x)^{m} = v(\pi_0)^{k},$$ the left-hand side being a natural power and the right-hand side an integer power in the linearly ordered commutative group with zero `O.ValueGroup` (legitimate since $v(\pi_0) \neq 0$). In words: in the value group of any valuation on a field algebraic over $\mathbb{Q}$, the value of an arbitrary nonzero element is commensurable with the value of any fixed element of nonzero value strictly less than $1$; no claim of uniqueness of $m$ or $k$, and no bound on them, is made.
--
--   This is the statement that the value group of a valuation on an algebraic extension of $\mathbb{Q}$ is a torsion extension of the cyclic group generated by the value of a single element of value $<1$, so that such an element plays the role of a uniformiser up to commensurability. It is used in the study of place specialisations of modular curves, where $\pi_0$ is the chosen parameter at a node and the conclusion makes $v$ comparable to the $\pi_0$-adic valuation.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ValuationSubring_exists_valuation_pow_eq_valuation_zpow_of_isAlgebraic.lean

